            {
              "id": "7fc2bb6e-3307-3e57-b677-1744cb53e42b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be risky and can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `std::memmove` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using risky C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. For instance, instead of `std::memmove`, consider using `std::copy` or `std::copy_n` from `<algorithm>` which are safer as they work with iterators and can take advantage of C++ type safety.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix using `std::copy_n`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// Assuming get(p) returns a pointer to Char and width and n are of appropriate types\nstd::copy_n(get(p), n, get(p) + (width - n) / 2);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `std::memmove`\n\nThe fixed code requires the following library:\n\n- `<algorithm>` for `std::copy_n`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "25fd99d3-32b0-3ddd-88e1-6d716ebed015",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. This function is used to calculate the length of a string, but it can lead to a buffer overflow if the string is not null-terminated. This can happen if the string is read from an untrusted source without proper validation or sanitization. The buffer overflow can lead to unexpected behavior, crashes, or even code execution, depending on the context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character (`'\\0'`) at the end, or by manually adding one before calling `strlen`.\n\nIn addition, you should always validate and sanitize your inputs, especially if they come from an untrusted source. This can help prevent buffer overflows and other types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nif (value != nullptr) {\n    size_t len = 0;\n    while (value[len] != '\\0') {\n        ++len;\n    }\n    str = {value, len};\n} else {\n    str = {value, 0};\n}\n```\n\nThis code manually calculates the length of the string, stopping when it encounters a null character. This avoids the potential buffer overflow that could occur with `strlen`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `std::strlen`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible. Always check the validity of the links before using them."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3cdea013-5041-3fc2-9631-8e6f534b64ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen`, use `strnlen`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nconst char* str_value = \"example\";\nstd::size_t max_length = 100; // Maximum expected length of the string\nstr = { str_value, std::strnlen(str_value, max_length) };\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "51590e07-6e15-3d90-a6db-ae9bfaf07c9a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential for causing buffer overflow. In C++, this often includes functions like `strcpy()`, `sprintf()`, `vsprintf()`, `gets()`, etc. These functions do not perform bounds checking, and can therefore overwrite memory, leading to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, or code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `vsnprintf()` is used, which is generally considered safer than `sprintf()` or `vsprintf()`, because it takes a size argument to prevent buffer overflows. However, it can still be misused or cause issues if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives whenever possible. For example, in C++, you can use `std::string` and `std::stringstream` for string manipulation, which automatically handle memory management and prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `vsnprintf()` function is used correctly, as it includes the size of the buffer as an argument. However, to further improve the safety of the code, you could consider using `std::vsnprintf()` instead, which is a safer version of `vsnprintf()`.\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdarg>\n#include <cstdio>\n\nvoid log(const char* format, ...) {\n    char logbuf[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    std::vsnprintf(logbuf, sizeof(logbuf), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // log the message\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdarg>`: for handling variable argument lists\n- `<cstdio>`: for the `vsnprintf()` function\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "17ce7480-d04b-365e-9f8a-5b755ce694ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For `printf`, a safer alternative is `snprintf`, which includes a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows. Also, always control the format string and never pass user input directly to it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf` function with `snprintf` function:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3d8e78ed-41db-366a-bb04-0e50cae4a457",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. One such function is `fprintf`.\n\nThe `fprintf` function can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. It can cause format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not a constant, or if it's composed dynamically, it can lead to buffer overflows if the output is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. These functions include the size of the buffer as an argument, which can prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fprintf`, use `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // Now buffer contains the formatted string.\n    // You can write it to file with fwrite or similar\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5bab92c4-dd0d-3d25-9b04-22019fba6bac",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Avoid Storing Tokens or Keys in Source Code Vulnerability\" is a common security issue in C++ programming where sensitive data such as API keys, tokens, or passwords are hard-coded directly into the source code. This is a bad practice as it exposes these sensitive data to potential attackers who gain access to the source code. This vulnerability can lead to unauthorized access to systems or services, data breaches, and other security incidents.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid storing sensitive data directly in the source code. Instead, use environment variables, configuration files, or secure vault services to store and access these data. These methods provide better security as they allow sensitive data to be stored separately from the source code and can be secured independently.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code example \"8081828384858687888990919293949596979899\" does not seem to be a valid C++ code, and it's unclear how it relates to the vulnerability. However, here is a general example of how to fix this vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n// Bad practice\nstd::string apiKey = \"1234567890\";\n\n// Good practice\nstd::string apiKey = std::getenv(\"API_KEY\");\n```\n\nIn the good practice example, the API key is stored as an environment variable named \"API_KEY\", and the `std::getenv` function is used to retrieve its value.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `cstdlib` library is required for the `std::getenv` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS173237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a8f8ca97-014d-3872-8952-fbfebda175bd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Avoid Storing Tokens or Keys in Source Code Vulnerability\" is a common security issue in C++ programming where sensitive data such as API keys, tokens, or passwords are hard-coded directly into the source code. This is a bad practice as it exposes these sensitive data to potential attackers who gain access to the source code. This vulnerability can lead to unauthorized access to systems or services, data breaches, and other security incidents.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid storing sensitive data directly in the source code. Instead, use secure methods to store and retrieve these data, such as environment variables, secure server configurations, or encrypted databases. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink \"6061626364656667686970717273747576777879\" appears to be a hard-coded hexadecimal string. If this string represents sensitive data, it should be removed from the source code. \n\nInstead, you could store this sensitive data in an environment variable and retrieve it in your C++ code as follows:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for std::getenv\n\n// ...\n\nconst char* token = std::getenv(\"TOKEN\");\nif (token == nullptr) {\n    // Handle error: The TOKEN environment variable was not set.\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `std::getenv` is used to retrieve the value of the \"TOKEN\" environment variable. If the environment variable is not set, `std::getenv` returns a null pointer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the `<cstdlib>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
