                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "88260ca0-9e15-3735-a958-a77f2d6b330d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Avoid Storing Tokens or Keys in Source Code Vulnerability\" is a common security issue in C++ programming where sensitive data such as API keys, tokens, or passwords are hard-coded directly into the source code. This is a bad practice as it exposes these sensitive data to potential attackers who gain access to the source code. This vulnerability can lead to unauthorized access to systems or services, data breaches, and other security incidents.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid storing sensitive data directly in the source code. Instead, use secure methods such as environment variables, secure server configurations, or encrypted databases to store and access these data. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink \"4041424344454647484950515253545556575859\" appears to be a hard-coded string. If this is a sensitive token or key, it should be removed from the source code. \n\nInstead, you could use an environment variable to store this value. Here's an example of how you could modify your code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // Required for getenv\n\n// ...\n\nconst char* token = std::getenv(\"TOKEN\");\nif (!token) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, \"TOKEN\" is the name of the environment variable that stores your sensitive data. \n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library for the `std::getenv` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
