            },
            {
              "id": "627b4d3d-f542-3e89-a48c-079837ab7c78",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe or insecure. In this case, the function `localtime()` is identified as a potential vulnerability sink. The `localtime()` function in C++ is used to convert a given time since epoch to calendar time expressed in local time. The problem with `localtime()` is that it is not thread-safe, meaning it can lead to data races and undefined behavior if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a safer alternative function that is thread-safe. In C++, the `localtime_s()` function is a safer alternative to `localtime()`. The `localtime_s()` function is part of the C11 standard and is designed to be thread-safe.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can replace `localtime()` with `localtime_s()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\n// Old vulnerable code\n// struct tm * timeinfo = localtime(&now_t);\n\n// New secure code\nstruct tm timeinfo;\nlocaltime_s(&timeinfo, &now_t);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<ctime>` library.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f2c1d346-085b-321e-8769-7dd5fdad4238",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions within C++ code. These functions are often older and do not include protections against common security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows or format string vulnerabilities. \n\nIn this case, the function `localtime` is considered insecure. The `localtime` function converts a time_t value to a tm structure as local time. It returns a pointer to static data and hence isn't thread-safe. If the function is called from multiple threads, the data can be overwritten leading to incorrect results.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `localtime`, a safer alternative is `localtime_r`, which is a reentrant version of `localtime`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace `localtime` with `localtime_r`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <time.h>\n\nvoid printLocalTime(time_t timer) {\n    struct tm result;\n    localtime_r(&timer, &result);\n    printf(\"%s\", asctime(&result));\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `localtime_r` is used instead of `localtime`. The second argument to `localtime_r` is a pointer to a `tm` structure that `localtime_r` fills in with the broken-down time.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- time.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dc61fd84-f978-309b-8d19-62d1bc17f59d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard library function in C++ for converting a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not perform any error checking. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform error checking, such as `strtol()` or `sscanf()`. Also, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(argv[3])` with the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[3], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[3] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- cstdlib\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
