            {
              "id": "b1aad74d-cd67-3414-9013-75fc7b95d77b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The code copies data from one memory location to another using a standard memory copy function, but there is evidence that the source and destination may overlap, which is not allowed for this function and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there is no check to ensure that the source pointer is not null, which could also lead to a crash or further undefined behavior. The destination is not a pointer-based member, so it is less likely to be null, but the source pointer is not guaranteed to be valid. These factors indicate a real risk that should be addressed to ensure program correctness and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. You can add a check to verify that the memory ranges are either completely separate or use a function that safely handles overlapping regions. For example:\n\n```c\nif ((char *)&v + sizeof(v) <= (char *)ptr || (char *)ptr + sizeof(v) <= (char *)&v) {\n    memcpy(&v, ptr, sizeof(v));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use memmove or adjust logic\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before calling the memory copy function.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible or cannot be ruled out, use a memory move function that is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example:\n\n```c\nmemmove(&v, ptr, sizeof(v));\n```\nThis function will correctly handle the case where the source and destination memory regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "db1b1936-3280-3919-8cbd-9cbbc2534adb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including denial of service, data corruption, and arbitrary code execution. This is why the use of `strcpy` is often prohibited in secure coding guidelines.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than a specified number of characters are copied to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name,\n        (name + std::to_string(i)).c_str(),\n        sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1);\ninfo->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name[sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` copies at most `sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1` characters from the source string to the destination buffer, and then manually null-terminates the destination string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strncpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::to_string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f9cd994d-b97f-354d-b33d-09de4e2cb17b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as 'UB Risk: possible src/dst overlap', and the verdict is 'GENUINE'. The code copies memory from one location to another using a standard memory copy function, but there is a risk that the source and destination memory regions may overlap. This can lead to undefined behavior, which is a serious correctness and security issue. The analysis indicates that overlap is feasible due to data flow between the source and destination, and there is also a risk that the source pointer may be null, as there is no prior check ensuring it is valid. The destination is not at risk of being null, but the overlap and null source risks make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are either completely separate or use a function that safely handles overlapping regions if overlap is possible.\n\n```c\nif ((char *)&v + sizeof(v) <= (char *)ptr || (char *)ptr + sizeof(v) <= (char *)&v) {\n    memcpy(&v, ptr, sizeof(v));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use memmove or adjust logic\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between the source and destination cannot be ruled out, use a memory move function that is designed to handle overlapping regions safely:\n\n```c\nmemmove(&v, ptr, sizeof(v));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d66a9190-f7ba-3eb9-92db-acccea53c65d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a function that can lead to a security risk. In C++, certain functions are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, integer overflows, or other types of vulnerabilities. One such function is `atoi()`, which converts a string to an integer. This function does not perform any error checking, so if the string cannot be converted into an integer, it will return a zero or a negative value, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `atoi()` and instead use functions that perform error checking, such as `strtol()`. This function allows you to specify the base for the conversion and it sets `errno` to `ERANGE` if the number cannot be represented.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(argv[1])` with the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n#include <climits>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong val = strtol(argv[1], &endptr, 10);\n\nif ((errno == ERANGE && (val == LONG_MAX || val == LONG_MIN)) || (errno != 0 && val == 0)) {\n    perror(\"strtol\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n\nif (endptr == argv[1]) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"No digits were found\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- cstdlib\n- cerrno\n- climits\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fa429605-2089-346a-9746-9394043c239e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the data flow analysis. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer could be null, since there is no prior check ensuring it is valid. The use of a memory copy function without overlap protection in such a scenario can lead to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities. The absence of explicit bounds checks or null pointer guards further increases the risk. Developers should ensure that the source and destination do not overlap and that the source pointer is valid before performing the copy.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap and that the source pointer is not null. Add explicit checks to prevent undefined behavior:\n\n```c\nif (ptr != NULL && ((char *)&v + sizeof(v) <= (char *)ptr || (char *)ptr + sizeof(v) <= (char *)&v)) {\n    memcpy(&v, ptr, sizeof(v));\n} else {\n    // Handle error: null pointer or overlapping regions\n}\n```\nThis code checks that the source pointer is not null and that the memory regions do not overlap before calling the memory copy function.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible or cannot be ruled out, use a memory move function that safely handles overlapping regions:\n\n```c\nif (ptr != NULL) {\n    memmove(&v, ptr, sizeof(v));\n} else {\n    // Handle error: null pointer\n}\n```\nThis approach ensures that even if the source and destination overlap, the operation will not result in undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ae91475c-4abc-37bb-8828-2c0b19b1eccd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the destination pointer used in the string copy operation may be null, and there are no visible checks in the code to ensure it is valid before use. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the absence of a guard for the destination pointer means that if it is null at runtime, the program will exhibit undefined behavior, potentially leading to a crash or security issue. The risk is further confirmed by the explicit classification and verdict indicating a genuine issue. Developers should always validate pointers before using them in memory or string operations to prevent undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the string copy operation, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the pointer is invalid.\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->pcie_status.pcie_bus_id) {\n    strncpy(status->pcie_status.pcie_bus_id, \"0000:59:00.0\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that both the `status` structure and the `pcie_bus_id` buffer are valid before attempting to copy the string. If either is null, the copy operation is skipped, preventing a potential crash or undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ac4d7e55-a131-3b43-a694-6588fd134ce3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a call to strncpy. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there is a risk that either the source or destination pointer could be null, as there are no clear guards in place to prevent this. The function does not use a count value derived from the destination's capacity, and there is no evidence of bounds checking or overlap prevention. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue that could lead to memory corruption or program crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. This can be done by comparing the memory addresses and lengths of the buffers. If overlap is detected, handle the case appropriately (e.g., skip the copy, log an error, or use a safe copy method).\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src = event_buffer_get_message(event_buffer, i);\nchar* dst = event_record.message;\nsize_t count = AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR;\n\nif ((src + count <= dst) || (dst + count <= src)) {\n    strncpy(dst, src, count);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap: log error, use safe copy, or skip\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping regions. Note that `memmove` does not NUL-terminate strings, so you may need to add a NUL terminator manually if working with strings.\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src = event_buffer_get_message(event_buffer, i);\nchar* dst = event_record.message;\nsize_t count = AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR;\n\nmemmove(dst, src, count);\ndst[count - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure NUL-termination if needed\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c605d40e-a398-38bb-a920-4eacf3fbdc4f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there is a possibility that either the source or destination pointer could be null, which would also result in undefined behavior. No explicit checks or guards are present to prevent these issues. The use of a constant for the copy length does not mitigate the risk of overlap or null pointer dereference. Therefore, this is a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before calling the copy function. Also, verify that neither pointer is null to prevent undefined behavior. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (event.message != nullptr && event_buffer_get_message(event_buffer, i) != nullptr &&\n    (event.message + AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR <= event_buffer_get_message(event_buffer, i) ||\n     event_buffer_get_message(event_buffer, i) + AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR <= event.message)) {\n    strncpy(event.message, event_buffer_get_message(event_buffer, i), AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    event.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the source and destination do not overlap and are not null before performing the copy.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Also, check for null pointers before copying:\n\n```cpp\nif (event.message != nullptr && event_buffer_get_message(event_buffer, i) != nullptr) {\n    memmove(event.message, event_buffer_get_message(event_buffer, i), AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    event.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior due to overlapping memory and null pointers.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "19effa70-07b4-3e29-aa7b-ce0cbe89adf1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the destination pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null checks before the buffer operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as genuine. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the destination pointer could be null, leading to a potential crash or undefined behavior if the function is called with an invalid pointer. There are no explicit guards or null-termination after the copy, and the code does not ensure the destination is valid before use.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy operation, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the pointer is invalid.\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->card_sku) {\n    strncpy(status->card_sku, \"D65205\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->card_sku[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis prevents the function from attempting to write to a null pointer and also ensures the destination string is null-terminated, which is a good practice when using `strncpy`.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the structure or pointer may be allocated elsewhere, consider validating all pointers at the start of the function or before any buffer operation. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (!status || !status->card_sku) {\n    // Handle error: invalid pointer\n    return;\n}\nstrncpy(status->card_sku, \"D65205\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nstatus->card_sku[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\nThis approach makes the function robust against invalid input and prevents undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
