            {
              "id": "79eb9c63-cdd5-31cd-a94c-111eb8176911",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the destination or source pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null pointer checks before the buffer operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The source is a string literal, which is always non-null, but the destination pointer may be null, as there is no evidence of a guard or check ensuring it is valid. The absence of a null-terminator assignment after the copy further increases the risk. The probability of this being a false positive is very low, as the analysis found no mitigating factors such as pointer validation or explicit null checks.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy operation, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the pointer is invalid.\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->vbios_part_number) {\n    strncpy(status->vbios_part_number, \"113-D65205-107\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->vbios_part_number[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach prevents dereferencing a null pointer and ensures the destination buffer is always null-terminated after the copy.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the structure or buffer allocation is not guaranteed elsewhere, consider initializing or allocating the destination buffer before use. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (!status) {\n    status = (StatusType*)malloc(sizeof(StatusType));\n    // Initialize status fields as needed\n}\nif (status->vbios_part_number) {\n    strncpy(status->vbios_part_number, \"113-D65205-107\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->vbios_part_number[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures the destination buffer is valid before copying data into it.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
