            {
              "id": "fb69edd5-3067-339c-8303-b3c65b3c800c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string literal into a destination buffer using a function that does not perform null pointer checks. The analysis did not detect any guard or validation to ensure that the destination pointer is not null before the copy operation. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the destination pointer could be null, leading to a potential crash or undefined behavior. The absence of a null check for the destination pointer is a significant risk factor.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string literal into the destination buffer, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior:\n\n```c++\nif (status && status->memory_vendor) {\n    strncpy(status->memory_vendor, \"hynix\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->memory_vendor[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the destination pointer is valid before performing the copy, and also guarantees that the destination buffer is null-terminated, which is a best practice when using `strncpy`.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the structure or pointer may be null, consider returning an error or handling the case gracefully to avoid dereferencing a null pointer:\n\n```c++\nif (!status || !status->memory_vendor) {\n    // Handle error: invalid pointer\n    return ERROR_INVALID_POINTER;\n}\nstrncpy(status->memory_vendor, \"hynix\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nstatus->memory_vendor[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n```\nThis approach prevents the function from proceeding with invalid pointers and allows for safer error handling.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
