            {
              "id": "e26f2af5-dd5f-3cb5-b79a-0e617a379c7f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the size parameter. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, alter the intended control flow, read sensitive information, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null-terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `strlcpy` or `std::string`. Here is an example of how to do it with `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string event_message(event_record.message);\nif (event_message.size() > AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR) {\n    event_message.resize(AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b306b931-7bde-3ef4-8c33-14b9afe63cfc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code copies a string literal into a destination buffer using a function that does not guarantee null-termination if the buffer size is reached, and there are no visible checks to ensure that the destination pointer is not null before the operation. The risk is further increased because the destination pointer may be null, which could lead to undefined behavior or a crash. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the absence of a null check on the destination pointer is a significant concern. No explicit null-termination is performed after the copy, which could also lead to string handling issues if the buffer is filled to capacity. The analysis did not detect any overlap between source and destination, and the source cannot be null, but the destination pointer's validity is not guaranteed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the destination pointer is not null to prevent undefined behavior. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after the copy to avoid unterminated strings if the buffer is filled to capacity.\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->vbios_version) {\n    strncpy(status->vbios_version, \"022.040.003.041.000001\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->vbios_version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis approach prevents dereferencing a null pointer and ensures the string is always null-terminated, even if the source string is longer than the buffer.\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3ac63cc4-461d-3272-b5fe-d4d5aa11f114",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the destination pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null checks before the buffer operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the destination pointer could be null, leading to a potential crash or undefined behavior if the function is called with a null pointer. There are no explicit guards or null-termination after the copy, and the length argument is not derived from the actual buffer capacity, increasing the risk. The absence of overlap risk and the fact that the source cannot be null slightly reduce the likelihood of a false positive, but the lack of null checks on the destination is a critical issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy operation, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. This prevents undefined behavior or crashes if the function is called with a null pointer. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->card_series) {\n    strncpy(status->card_series, \"AMD INSTINCT MI200 (MCM) OAM AC MBA MSFT\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->card_series[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the destination buffer is valid before copying, and also explicitly null-terminates the string to avoid unterminated strings if the source is longer than the buffer size.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the structure containing the destination buffer is always expected to be allocated, but the buffer itself could be uninitialized, consider initializing the structure and its members before use. Additionally, always null-terminate the destination buffer after using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure status is allocated and card_series is a valid buffer before this point\nstrncpy(status->card_series, \"AMD INSTINCT MI200 (MCM) OAM AC MBA MSFT\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nstatus->card_series[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Null-terminate\n```\nThis approach assumes that the structure and its members are properly initialized elsewhere in the code.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
