            {
              "id": "a6ac8cb8-43fc-3b71-a13f-d0ee51049394",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the absence of null pointer checks for the destination buffer. The code copies a string literal into a destination buffer using a function that does not guarantee null-termination if the buffer size is reached, and there is no evidence of a guard to ensure the destination pointer is not null before the operation. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the destination pointer may be null, as there are no checks in place. This can lead to a crash or other undefined behavior if the destination pointer is ever null at runtime. The probability of this being a false positive is very low due to the lack of null checks and the genuine risk of dereferencing a null pointer.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string literal into the destination buffer, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->serial_num) {\n    strncpy(status->serial_num, \"PCB046982-0071\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->serial_num[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents dereferencing a null pointer and also ensures the destination buffer is null-terminated, which is important when using `strncpy`.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the structure or buffer allocation is guaranteed elsewhere, document and assert this assumption to catch programming errors early:\n\n```cpp\nassert(status != NULL && status->serial_num != NULL);\nstrncpy(status->serial_num, \"PCB046982-0071\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nstatus->serial_num[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\nThis will cause the program to fail fast in debug builds if the assumption is violated, making it easier to detect issues during development.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
