            {
              "id": "d831dc05-dce8-36c3-aeb1-1f6aaa3249a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination pointers are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer sizes and the lack of explicit null-termination after the copy further increase the risk. These factors indicate a real and actionable security issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the pointers are not null and that the regions are distinct. For example:\n\n```c\nif (fw_version && version && (fw_version->version != version)) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, version, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the pointers are valid and do not overlap. It also guarantees null-termination of the destination buffer.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be supported, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping regions. For example:\n\n```c\nif (fw_version && version) {\n    memmove(fw_version->version, version, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures safe copying even if the source and destination overlap, and also adds null-termination to the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1ae5108e-ce58-3fa6-9b6d-a4812b36c401",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including denial of service, data corruption, and arbitrary code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `snprintf`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of characters are copied to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string source = name + std::to_string(gpu1->id());\nstrncpy(info->peer_device[walk_ctxt->count].peer_device.name, source.c_str(), sizeof(info->peer_device[walk_ctxt->count].peer_device.name) - 1);\ninfo->peer_device[walk_ctxt->count].peer_device.name[sizeof(info->peer_device[walk_ctxt->count].peer_device.name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn this fix, `strncpy` is used instead of `strcpy`, and the size of the destination buffer is passed as the third argument to ensure that the function does not write past the end of the buffer. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated, as `strncpy` does not null-terminate the string if the source string is longer than the specified length.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strcpy` and `strncpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::to_string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ec2e17f4-c403-3082-be15-2c709d2f7587",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string literal into a destination buffer using a function that does not check for null pointers. There is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure that the destination pointer is valid before the operation. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the destination may be null, as indicated by the analysis. This can lead to a crash or other unpredictable behavior if the destination pointer is not properly initialized. The absence of a null-termination guarantee (no explicit null character written after the copy) further increases the risk of incorrect string handling.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add a check to verify that `status` and `status->driver_version` are valid pointers before calling the copy function. Also, ensure that the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->driver_version) {\n    strncpy(status->driver_version, \"6.3.6\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->driver_version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference and ensures the string is always null-terminated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the structure or buffer allocation is not guaranteed elsewhere, consider initializing or allocating the destination buffer before use. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (!status) {\n    // handle error or allocate status\n}\nif (!status->driver_version) {\n    status->driver_version = (char*)calloc(AGA_MAX_STR_LEN, sizeof(char));\n    if (!status->driver_version) {\n        // handle allocation failure\n    }\n}\nstrncpy(status->driver_version, \"6.3.6\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nstatus->driver_version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis ensures the destination buffer is valid and prevents null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cd7b2a2a-fae8-38df-986d-eaa52c70c3e8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can lead to unpredictable results. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination are non-null, which increases the risk of null pointer dereference. No explicit buffer size or null-termination handling is present, and there are no guards to prevent these issues. The classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the source and destination pointers are not the same or overlapping. Also, check that neither pointer is null before calling strncpy.\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && block &&\n    ((fw_version->firmware + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= block) ||\n     (block + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= fw_version->firmware))) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->firmware, block, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->firmware[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the memory regions do not overlap and that both pointers are valid.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and cannot be ruled out, use memmove instead of strncpy, as memmove safely handles overlapping regions. Also, ensure null-termination if required.\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && block) {\n    memmove(fw_version->firmware, block, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->firmware[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis change eliminates the undefined behavior risk associated with overlapping memory regions.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
