            {
              "id": "b04d94bb-6d8f-302e-a108-4cc40d5076fa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The code copies data from one buffer to another using a memory copy function, but there is evidence that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which can cause unpredictable results. Additionally, there are no checks to ensure that the source and destination pointers are not null, increasing the risk of a crash or further undefined behavior. The absence of explicit bounds checks or null pointer guards further increases the risk. This issue should be addressed to prevent potential security and stability problems.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, add explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Also, verify that neither pointer is null to prevent crashes. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && board_info.manufacturer_name &&\n    (status->card_vendor != board_info.manufacturer_name) &&\n    !((status->card_vendor <= board_info.manufacturer_name &&\n        status->card_vendor + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN > board_info.manufacturer_name) ||\n      (board_info.manufacturer_name <= status->card_vendor &&\n        board_info.manufacturer_name + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN > status->card_vendor))) {\n    memcpy(status->card_vendor, board_info.manufacturer_name, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the source and destination do not overlap and are not null before copying.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination buffers may overlap, use a memory move function that is safe for overlapping regions. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && board_info.manufacturer_name) {\n    memmove(status->card_vendor, board_info.manufacturer_name, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n}\n```\nThis approach ensures correct behavior even if the memory regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
