            {
              "id": "e625a29e-e0ba-3b4f-a4e1-b6d216db1154",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the absence of null pointer checks for the destination buffer. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The destination buffer is accessed via a pointer, and there is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure it is not null before use. If the destination pointer is null, calling strncpy will result in undefined behavior, potentially leading to a crash or security vulnerability. The source is a string generated by std::to_string, which is safe, but the main risk is the unchecked destination pointer.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the function that copies the string, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check for the destination structure and its buffer member:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && fw_version->version) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, std::to_string(version).c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination:\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the destination is valid before use. Also, consider always null-terminating the destination buffer after strncpy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the destination pointer can be null due to logic elsewhere, handle the error gracefully by returning an error code or logging the issue:\n\n```cpp\nif (!fw_version || !fw_version->version) {\n    // Handle error: log, return error code, or throw exception\n    return ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT;\n}\nstrncpy(fw_version->version, std::to_string(version).c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nfw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n```\nThis approach ensures the function fails safely if the destination is invalid.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
