            {
              "id": "60ab2554-aa24-321b-8e14-ca72e9dfa880",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination pointers are non-null, which increases the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer boundaries and the lack of explicit null-termination after the copy further increase the risk. The use of a named constant for the copy length does not guarantee safety if the underlying buffer sizes are not properly managed. All these factors contribute to a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be remediated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the memory regions are distinct and that neither pointer is null. Also, ensure that the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf &&\n    ((fw_version->version + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= buf) || (buf + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= fw_version->version))) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the buffers do not overlap and that the destination is always null-terminated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination buffers may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. After copying, ensure the destination is null-terminated.\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf) {\n    memmove(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures safe copying even if the buffers overlap, and guarantees null-termination.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b57e4f75-1e44-35a1-b675-af467c4557f2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to ensure the destination pointer is not null, which further increases the risk. The source pointer is confirmed to be non-null before the call, but the destination pointer may be null, and the overlap risk is present. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, add a check to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use a safe copy method or handle the overlap explicitly:\n\n```cpp\nif ((api_spec->id + SDK_MIN(proto_key.length(), OBJ_MAX_KEY_LEN) <= proto_key.data()) ||\n    (proto_key.data() + SDK_MIN(proto_key.length(), OBJ_MAX_KEY_LEN) <= api_spec->id)) {\n    memcpy(api_spec->id, proto_key.data(), SDK_MIN(proto_key.length(), OBJ_MAX_KEY_LEN));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use memmove or copy to a temporary buffer\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination is possible, replace the memcpy call with memmove, which is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(api_spec->id, proto_key.data(), SDK_MIN(proto_key.length(), OBJ_MAX_KEY_LEN));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bfce8c40-2307-3c2f-b19f-e118228db003",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory areas, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer may be null at this location, as there is no prior check ensuring it is valid. The destination is not likely to be null, but the overlap and null source risks make this a high-confidence issue. No explicit bounds or null-termination protections are present, and the operation uses a type-based size, which does not mitigate the overlap risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are either completely separate or use a function that safely handles overlapping regions if overlap is possible.\n\n```cpp\nif ((char*)&stats_ + sizeof(aga_gpu_watch_fields_t) <= (char*)stats ||\n    (char*)stats + sizeof(aga_gpu_watch_fields_t) <= (char*)&stats_) {\n    memcpy(&stats_, stats, sizeof(aga_gpu_watch_fields_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf there is a possibility that the source and destination memory regions overlap, use the `memmove` function instead of `memcpy`. The `memmove` function is designed to safely handle overlapping memory regions.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(&stats_, stats, sizeof(aga_gpu_watch_fields_t));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cb85af0b-edba-3210-b408-81218a4969cd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, crashes, and code execution. This is why the use of `strcpy` is often prohibited in secure coding guidelines.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `strncpy` function, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to be copied from the source string.\n\nHowever, `strncpy` can also lead to issues if not used correctly. If the source string is larger than the specified size, `strncpy` will not null-terminate the destination string, which can lead to other vulnerabilities. Therefore, it is important to always manually null-terminate the destination string when using `strncpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info->device.name, device_name.c_str(), sizeof(info->device.name) - 1);\ninfo->device.name[sizeof(info->device.name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65afbd2f-129c-393c-a5a1-afd82301de98",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the absence of null pointer checks for either the source or destination buffer. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The destination buffer is accessed via a pointer, and there is no evidence of a guard to ensure it is not null before use. Additionally, the destination buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, and the function does not guarantee that the destination will be null-terminated after the copy. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe, but the main risk is dereferencing a potentially null destination pointer, which can lead to a crash or other undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the copy operation, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. This can be done by adding an explicit check before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version != nullptr && fw_version->firmware != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->firmware, block_name.c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->firmware[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis prevents undefined behavior by avoiding dereferencing a null pointer and ensures the destination buffer is always null-terminated after the copy.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the destination pointer can be null due to logic elsewhere, consider returning an error or handling the case explicitly:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version == nullptr || fw_version->firmware == nullptr) {\n    // Handle error: destination is not valid\n    return ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT;\n}\nstrncpy(fw_version->firmware, block_name.c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nfw_version->firmware[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\nThis approach makes the function more robust and prevents silent failures or crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2a6203a2-0eaa-3a21-9c68-b45d26f609ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The dataflow analysis indicates that the destination buffer may alias or overlap with the source, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, the source pointer may be null at this call site, further increasing the risk. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the destination is a pointer type without a known fixed capacity. These factors all contribute to the high confidence in this being a real and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure the source and destination do not overlap before calling memcpy. If overlap is possible, use memmove instead, which is safe for overlapping regions.\n\n```cpp\nif ((spec != &spec_) &&\n    ((char*)spec + sizeof(aga_gpu_spec_t) <= (char*)&spec_ ||\n     (char*)&spec_ + sizeof(aga_gpu_spec_t) <= (char*)spec)) {\n    memcpy(spec, &spec_, sizeof(aga_gpu_spec_t));\n} else {\n    // Use memmove if overlap is possible\n    memmove(spec, &spec_, sizeof(aga_gpu_spec_t));\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, directly replace the memcpy call with memmove, which is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(spec, &spec_, sizeof(aga_gpu_spec_t));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
