            {
              "id": "1240a99e-b466-3d1b-b255-eab9ebb39248",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the size parameter. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, alter the intended control flow, read sensitive information, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null-terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `strlcpy` or `std::string`. Here is an example of how to do it with `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string event_message(event_record.message);\nif (event_message.size() > AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR) {\n    event_message.resize(AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "db6f45c6-f0ce-3105-bc47-be5e37fb6c31",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including denial of service, data corruption, and arbitrary code execution. This is why the use of `strcpy` is often prohibited in secure coding guidelines.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than a specified number of characters are copied to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name,\n        (name + std::to_string(i)).c_str(),\n        sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1);\ninfo->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name[sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` copies at most `sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1` characters from the source string to the destination buffer, and then manually null-terminates the destination string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strncpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::to_string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5da22c50-53dd-3d81-b274-3f2ce9fc19c9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior with memcpy. Additionally, the source may be null, which further increases the risk. There are no explicit guards or checks to prevent these issues, and the verdict confirms this is a genuine issue. The destination is not a pointer-based member, reducing the risk of null destination, but the overlap and possible null source remain significant concerns.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure the source and destination do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, use a safe alternative.\n\n```cpp\nif ((char*)&key.id[0] + sizeof(host_id) <= (char*)&host_id ||\n    (char*)&host_id + sizeof(host_id) <= (char*)&key.id[0]) {\n    memcpy(&key.id[0], &host_id, sizeof(host_id));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use memmove\n    memmove(&key.id[0], &host_id, sizeof(host_id));\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, replace the memory copy function with a function that is safe for overlapping memory regions, such as memmove:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(&key.id[0], &host_id, sizeof(host_id));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6386dba5-6783-3a4b-8be8-99a853a532ae",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a possibility that either the source or destination pointers could be null, as there are no clear checks to prevent this. The code does not appear to use the destination's capacity to limit the copy, and the count argument is not directly tied to the buffer size. The presence of an explicit null-terminator assignment after the call does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk from overlapping memory. All these factors indicate a high likelihood of a real issue that could lead to memory corruption or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are distinct. If overlap is possible, avoid using strncpy and consider using memmove, which is safe for overlapping regions.\n\n```cpp\nif ((event.message + AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR <= event_buffer[i].message) ||\n    (event_buffer[i].message + AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR <= event.message)) {\n    strncpy(event.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    event.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case safely, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination is possible, replace strncpy with memmove, which is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Also, ensure null-termination if needed.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(event.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\nevent.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9e6e2425-c7c5-30c7-9f37-6d2168d01707",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer sizes and the lack of explicit null-termination after the copy further increase the risk. These factors indicate a real and actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the memory regions are distinct. Also, check that neither pointer is null before calling strncpy. For example:\n\n```c++\nif (status && buf && (status->vbios_part_number != buf)) {\n    strncpy(status->vbios_part_number, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->vbios_part_number[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the pointers are valid and do not overlap, and also guarantees null-termination of the destination buffer.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use memmove instead of strncpy, as memmove is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. For example:\n\n```c++\nif (status && buf) {\n    memmove(status->vbios_part_number, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->vbios_part_number[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the copy is performed safely even if the regions overlap, and also adds explicit null-termination.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a23b1a9e-05e0-351d-9395-4c5c2757c690",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by data flow, which means they may refer to overlapping memory areas. This can cause unpredictable results when using memory copy functions that do not support overlapping regions. Additionally, the analysis could not rule out the possibility that the source pointer is null, which further increases the risk. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the function verdict confirms this as a genuine issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are distinct:\n\n```c\nif ((&key.id[8] + sizeof(gpu_unique_id) <= (char *)&gpu_unique_id) ||\n    ((char *)&gpu_unique_id + sizeof(gpu_unique_id) <= &key.id[8])) {\n    memcpy(&key.id[8], &gpu_unique_id, sizeof(gpu_unique_id));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf there is a possibility that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use a memory move function that is safe for overlapping regions instead of a direct memory copy:\n\n```c\nmemmove(&key.id[8], &gpu_unique_id, sizeof(gpu_unique_id));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
