            {
              "id": "6e46fbca-14d8-3eec-a8cb-a6ca02519e9c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the destination pointer may be null at the point of the strncpy call. There are no visible checks in the code to ensure that the destination pointer is valid before use. The source is a string generated by std::to_string, which is always valid, so the risk is solely with the destination. The absence of a null check for the destination pointer means that if it is null, the program may crash or exhibit undefined behavior. The classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence issue. The probability of this being a false positive is very low, as the evidence points to a real risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the function that performs the copy, ensure that the destination pointer (fw_version) and its version member are not null. Add explicit checks to prevent undefined behavior:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version != nullptr && fw_version->version != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, std::to_string(version).c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination:\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis prevents the function from attempting to write to a null pointer, which would otherwise cause a crash or undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the function is responsible for allocating or validating the destination structure, add an early return or error handling if the pointer is null:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version == nullptr || fw_version->version == nullptr) {\n    // Handle error, log, or return an error code\n    return ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT;\n}\nstrncpy(fw_version->version, std::to_string(version).c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nfw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n```\nThis approach ensures that the function does not proceed with invalid pointers, improving robustness and security.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "31cca677-6581-308f-9ce7-78f57cafac97",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. The risk is further supported by dataflow analysis indicating a possible overlap. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this, and the destination buffer is a pointer type with no clear size information. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can also lead to string handling issues. The source and destination are both confirmed to be non-null at this point, but the overlap risk remains the primary concern.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd a runtime check to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before performing the string copy. This can be done by comparing the addresses and lengths of the buffers. If overlap is detected, handle the case appropriately (e.g., use a temporary buffer or abort the operation).\n\n```c++\nif ((sku + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= token) || (token + strlen(token) <= sku)) {\n    strncpy(sku, token, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap: use a temporary buffer or report an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as the memory move function, instead of the standard string copy function. This function is designed to handle overlapping memory safely.\n\n```c++\nsize_t len = strnlen(token, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nmemmove(sku, token, len);\nif (len < AGA_MAX_STR_LEN) {\n    sku[len] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1cd75952-7cae-3cab-ae2c-8ba19aa6b72c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf` which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflow. Additionally, never use user-supplied data as a format string. Always control the format string and use user-supplied data only as arguments to the format specifiers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fprintf` function with a safer alternative like `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example of how to do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), arg1, fmt::ArgList(fmt::internal::make_type(args...), array));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>` for `fprintf`, `snprintf`, `vsnprintf`\n- `<fmt/core.h>` for `fmt::ArgList`, `fmt::internal::make_type`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "92dfa36c-4340-335a-a2d6-d7a132d4ba90",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there is a risk that either the source or destination pointer could be null, as there are no clear checks or guards in place to prevent this. The function does not explicitly ensure that the destination buffer is large enough or that the source and destination do not overlap. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue that could lead to unpredictable program behavior or security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the copy operation, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the memory addresses and ranges are distinct. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif ((fw_version->firmware != gpu_fw_block_name_str_(block)) &&\n    ((fw_version->firmware + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= gpu_fw_block_name_str_(block)) ||\n     (gpu_fw_block_name_str_(block) + strlen(gpu_fw_block_name_str_(block)) <= fw_version->firmware))) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->firmware, gpu_fw_block_name_str_(block), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap error, e.g., log or use a safe copy method\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the source and destination do not overlap before performing the copy.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use a function that is safe for overlapping memory, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. For example:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(fw_version->firmware, gpu_fw_block_name_str_(block), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n```\nNote that `memmove` does not guarantee NUL-termination for strings, so you may need to manually set the last byte to '\\0' if required.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fa9e0e34-6adc-383e-a8e3-1510b4213ec2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. One such function is `fprintf`.\n\nThe `fprintf` function can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. It can cause format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not a constant, or if it's composed dynamically, it can lead to buffer overflows if the output is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. These functions include the size of the buffer as an argument, which can prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fprintf`, use `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // Now buffer contains the formatted string.\n    // You can write it to file with fwrite or similar\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "03101c76-87ae-392f-8b4e-03ed8979c416",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, which further increases the risk of runtime errors such as segmentation faults. The absence of a null-terminator assignment after the copy operation also means that the destination buffer may not be properly null-terminated, potentially leading to string handling errors. These factors collectively indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure program correctness and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the pointers do not reference overlapping memory. Also, check that neither pointer is null before the operation. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf &&\n    ((fw_version->version + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= buf) || (buf + strlen(buf) <= fw_version->version))) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs if the source and destination do not overlap and are both valid pointers. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as the memory move function. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf) {\n    memmove(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach safely handles overlapping memory regions and ensures the destination buffer is null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "998de115-6f83-368a-9a3d-09d99dd85b53",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination pointers are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer sizes or null checks further increases the severity of this issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the copy operation, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the pointers are not null and that the memory regions are distinct. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf && (fw_version->version != buf)) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the source and destination are not the same and are both valid pointers.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use a function that supports overlapping regions, such as memmove. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf) {\n    memmove(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination if needed\n}\n```\nThis ensures safe copying even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            }
